Your task is to create a Tooltip class that can be instantiated with different types of content and can be attached to specific elements on the web page. The Tooltip class should have the following functionalities:
1. Accept different types of content (text, icons, links) to be displayed in the tooltip.
2. Allow customization of the tooltip appearance, such as background color, text color, and position.
3. Attach the tooltip to specific elements on the web page, so that it appears when the user hovers over those elements.
4. Ensure that the tooltip disappears when the user moves the cursor away from the attached element.

You should also write a test suite using a testing framework of your choice to ensure that the Tooltip class functions as expected.

### Solution:
```javascript
class Tooltip {
  constructor(content, options) {
    this.content = content;
    this.options = options || {};
    this.tooltipElement = null;
    this.attachToElement = null;
  }

  createTooltipElement() {
    this.tooltipElement = document.createElement("div");
    this.tooltipElement.classList.add("tooltip");
    this.tooltipElement.innerHTML = this.content;
    // Apply custom styles from options
    if (this.options.backgroundColor) {
      this.tooltipElement.style.backgroundColor = this.options.backgroundColor;
    }
    if (this.options.textColor) {
      this.tooltipElement.style.color = this.options.textColor;
    }
    // Other style customizations can be added here

    document.body.appendChild(this.tooltipElement);
  }

  attach(element) {
    this.attachToElement = element;
    element.addEventListener("mouseenter", this.showTooltip.bind(this));
    element.addEventListener("mouseleave", this.hideTooltip.bind(this));
  }

  showTooltip() {
    if (!this.tooltipElement) {
      this.createTooltipElement();
    }
    // Position the tooltip relative to the attached element
    const elementRect = this.attachToElement.getBoundingClientRect();
    this.tooltipElement.style.top = `${elementRect.top + window.scrollY}px`;
    this.tooltipElement.style.left = `${elementRect.left + window.scrollX}px`;
    this.tooltipElement.style.position = "absolute";
  }

  hideTooltip() {
    if (this.tooltipElement) {
      document.body.removeChild(this.tooltipElement);
      this.tooltipElement = null;
    }
  }
}

// Test suite using Jest
describe("Tooltip", () => {
  let tooltip;
  let element;

  beforeEach(() => {
    tooltip = new Tooltip("Sample tooltip content", {
      backgroundColor: "lightgray",
      textColor: "black",
    });
    element = document.createElement("div");
    document.body.appendChild(element);
  });

  afterEach(() => {
    document.body.removeChild(element);
  });

  test("creates and shows tooltip on hover", () => {
    tooltip.attach(element);
    element.dispatchEvent(new Event("mouseenter"));
    expect(document.querySelector(".tooltip")).toBeTruthy();
  });

  test("hides tooltip on mouse leave", () => {
    tooltip.attach(element);
    element.dispatchEvent(new Event("mouseenter"));
    element.dispatchEvent(new Event("mouseleave"));
    expect(document.querySelector(".tooltip")).toBeFalsy();
  });
});
```

In this solution, the Tooltip class is implemented with the required functionalities, including creating the tooltip element, attaching it to specific elements, and handling its appearance and disappearance. Additionally, a test suite using Jest is provided to ensure the proper functioning of the Tooltip class.

