### Solution:
```csharp
using System;

// Define the Column class
public class Column
{
    public bool isClicked { get; set; }

    public event EventHandler IsClickedChanged;

    public void NotifyIsClickedChanged()
    {
        IsClickedChanged?.Invoke(this, EventArgs.Empty);
    }
}

// Define the Observer class
public class Observer
{
    public void Update(Column column)
    {
        if (column.isClicked)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Column is clicked");
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Column is unclicked");
        }
    }
}

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // Create instances of Column and Observer
        Column column = new Column();
        Observer observer = new Observer();

        // Subscribe the observer to the IsClickedChanged event of the column
        column.IsClickedChanged += (sender, args) => observer.Update(column);

        // Change the isClicked property of the column to trigger the observer
        column.isClicked = true;
        column.NotifyIsClickedChanged(); // This will trigger the observer to print "Column is clicked"

        column.isClicked = false;
        column.NotifyIsClickedChanged(); // This will trigger the observer to print "Column is unclicked"
    }
}
```

In this solution, we first define the `Column` class with a boolean property `isClicked` and an event `IsClickedChanged` which is raised when the `isClicked` property changes. We then define the `Observer` class with an `Update` method that prints the appropriate message based on the `isClicked` property of the `Column`. In the `Main` method, we create instances of `Column` and `Observer`, subscribe the observer to the `IsClickedChanged` event of the column, and then change the `isClicked` property to trigger the observer and see the messages printed based on the property changes.

