    // Your implementation here
}
```

You can assume that the necessary HTML and JavaScript setup for accessing the webcam and obtaining the video stream is already in place. The canvas element is also already created and available for use.

### Solution:
```javascript
function displayGrayscaleVideo(videoStream, canvasElement) {
    const videoElement = document.createElement('video');
    videoElement.srcObject = videoStream;
    videoElement.play();

    const canvasContext = canvasElement.getContext('2d');

    const drawVideoFrame = () => {
        canvasContext.drawImage(videoElement, 0, 0, canvasElement.width, canvasElement.height);
        const imageData = canvasContext.getImageData(0, 0, canvasElement.width, canvasElement.height);
        const data = imageData.data;

        for (let i = 0; i < data.length; i += 4) {
            const avg = (data[i] + data[i + 1] + data[i + 2]) / 3;
            data[i] = avg; // Red
            data[i + 1] = avg; // Green
            data[i + 2] = avg; // Blue
        }

        canvasContext.putImageData(imageData, 0, 0);
        requestAnimationFrame(drawVideoFrame);
    };

    videoElement.addEventListener('play', drawVideoFrame);
}
```

This solution creates a video element, sets its source to the provided video stream, and starts playing it. It then sets up a continuous drawing loop using requestAnimationFrame to update the canvas with the grayscale video frames. The frames are processed by converting each pixel to grayscale and updating the canvas with the modified image data.

