Your `PlayerOutOfWorldEvent` class should extend `PlayerEvent` and include the following:
- A constructor that takes a `Player` and a `Location` as parameters.
- A method to retrieve the player associated with the event.
- A method to retrieve the location where the player went out of bounds.

You should also demonstrate how to register the event in a Minecraft plugin and provide a sample usage scenario where the event is triggered when a player goes out of the world boundaries.

### Solution:
```java
import org.bukkit.Bukkit;
import org.bukkit.Location;
import org.bukkit.entity.Player;
import org.bukkit.event.EventHandler;
import org.bukkit.event.Listener;
import org.bukkit.event.player.PlayerMoveEvent;
import org.bukkit.plugin.java.JavaPlugin;

public class PlayerOutOfWorldEvent extends PlayerEvent {
    private final Location outOfBoundsLocation;

    public PlayerOutOfWorldEvent(Player player, Location outOfBoundsLocation) {
        super(player);
        this.outOfBoundsLocation = outOfBoundsLocation;
    }

    public Player getPlayer() {
        return this.getPlayer();
    }

    public Location getOutOfBoundsLocation() {
        return this.outOfBoundsLocation;
    }
}

class PlayerOutOfBoundsListener implements Listener {
    private final JavaPlugin plugin;

    public PlayerOutOfBoundsListener(JavaPlugin plugin) {
        this.plugin = plugin;
    }

    @EventHandler
    public void onPlayerMove(PlayerMoveEvent event) {
        Location playerLocation = event.getPlayer().getLocation();
        if (playerLocation.getY() < 0) {
            PlayerOutOfWorldEvent outOfWorldEvent = new PlayerOutOfWorldEvent(event.getPlayer(), playerLocation);
            Bukkit.getPluginManager().callEvent(outOfWorldEvent);
        }
    }
}

class YourPlugin extends JavaPlugin {
    @Override
    public void onEnable() {
        getServer().getPluginManager().registerEvents(new PlayerOutOfBoundsListener(this), this);
    }
}
```

